    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d452">

                <pb facs="0524" n="458" sameAs="frus:frus1934v05/pg458"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">411.12/1785: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Daniels</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-04-13T16:00:00-06:00">April 13, 1934—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:54 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>41. Referring to Department’s telegram No. 50, April 11, 11 a.m., Department’s
                    suggested addition to fourth paragraph of article No. IV is acceptable to
                    Foreign Office which in turn suggests the following addition to follow
                    Department’s paragraph: “In the latter event, the claims improperly eliminated
                    in the opinion of the Commissioners or Umpire, shall be settled and adjusted by
                    the same en bloc procedure prescribed by this Convention for all claims
                    registered with the Special Commission.”</p>
                <p>Foreign Office suggests another addition in said paragraph. Refer to enclosure 3,
                    despatch 1277 [<hi rendition="#italic">1267</hi>]<anchor n="91" xml:id="d452-f1"
                    /> page 3 line 26, after the words “the determination[s]”<anchor n="92"
                        xml:id="d452-f2"/> add “by the representatives of both Governments referred
                    to in article V of the Convention.” Line 30 should read “prejudge the
                    jurisdiction in, et cetera.”<anchor n="93" xml:id="d452-f3"/></p>
                <p>Regarding Department’s change in article I, Foreign Minister still considers that
                    it raises a new issue and increases Mexico’s anticipated estimated liability in
                    the settlement with the United States. Consequently he must consult President
                    before giving an answer which he hopes to do this afternoon.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Daniels</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
